## 3. Key Findings from 10,000 Simulated Runs

| Hospital Archetype | Unmitigated Breach Risk | Breach Risk With Filter | Annual Operating Cost | Median Net Savings | Median ROSI |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Community Hospital** | 1.70% | 0.03% | $58,348 | **$60,197** | **103.4%** |
| **Regional Hospital** | 15.65% | 0.34% | $212,992 | **$1,372,057** | **645.8%** |
| **Academic Medical Center** | 67.62% | 2.23% | $658,102 | **$7,575,861** | **1,149.7%** |

* **Regional Hospitals:** Without protection, a mid-sized facility faces a **15.65% chance of a breach** each year, representing about **$1.62 million in expected losses**. With the filter in place, breach likelihood drops to **0.34%**, producing a **median net annual savings of $1.37 million** and a **645.8% Return on Security Investment (ROSI)** even after covering clinician latency costs.
* **Academic Centers:** Due to massive staff sizes and high query volumes, large teaching hospitals experience a baseline breach risk of **67.62%**, where automated protection delivers a **median net savings of $7.58 million**.
* **Community Hospitals:** Smaller facilities maintain positive returns (**103.4% ROSI**), confirming that pre-prompt filtering is economically viable regardless of organizational scale.

